 social media has  kind of just like pushed me over the  edge  and then you know a few months ago I  think it's safe to say that I like  totally just burned out you know I hear  all the time from like friends and  family about how put together I look but  I really want to be transparent that  sometimes like that could not be farther  from the truth  that is just not reality folks and you  know my job while while working in the  media and blogging and youtubing and  making videos is like so much fun I love  my job and I'm so grateful for the  opportunities that I get to to put these  skills and my passion to use it is  really being hard to be in the public  eye all the time like really hard so my  job obviously is and always has been so  intimately tied to social media like I'm  I never off-the-grid  right like I never get a holiday I never  just get to punch out at the end of the  day or like tune everything out and  while a lot of you guys get to use  Instagram or YouTube for like  entertainment it is intimately tied up  in my own work and my own like  experience of success but like just  being online on Facebook and Instagram  and Twitter and YouTube all day long and  like you know feeling like you got to  respond to comments constantly and  always be checking up on things I I do  love it but it is so mentally exhausting  some days some days when you're already  tired and you're already down it's a lot  you know there's just so much pressure  on so-called influencers to always be  creating more content and better content  and constantly responding immediately as  if we can work all hours of the day it  doesn't matter if it's 2:00 in the  morning it weren't so response and we  can't slow down or else there's this  fear that we will just become obsolete  and no this actually has very little to  do with trolls for those of you who went  are wondering I actually have a pretty  level head about trolls and some days  when I'm feeling really good like I  always see it as a bit of a compliment  that somebody would spend you know hours  or days  like weeks out of there timed halfway  around the world thinking and talking  about me so that doesn't really bother  me all that much so while I totally get  it and I hear from influencers all the  time how you know emotionally exhausting  and devastating it can be to have to  deal with the public scrutiny at least  for me you know when I'm thinking with  the level head and not my insomnia  thoughts at 3:00 in the morning  it the trolls really don't usually  bother me all that much what I am  struggling with however is learning how  to just be enough you know how can i how  can I learn to just do what I can today  put it away until tomorrow and not let  my otherwise like amazing life be  overshadowed by my overwhelming drive to  just keep producing and more and making  more and working harder so honestly I  was just feeling like I needed to really  get my sleep under control get my  general anxiety under control and find a  better balance as my family and my  business grows you know I really would  love for my son to have a sibling one  day but I knew that that was just never  going to happen if I didn't prioritize  my well-being and ya know like getting  my sleep under control so that I wasn't  just gonna end up burning out and then  like crying all day like it's just not  productive  so as this like brave attempt at  self-care I decided to delete my social  media apps on my phone kind of is a bit  of an experiment to see what would  happen but also as a way to just give  myself a little bit of like  psychological rest I know that seems  like so easy and also kind of like so  silly even to be talking about or  thinking about but let's be honest with  ourselves like most Millennials are  completely addicted to their phones and  their social media apps not to mention I  like depend on these things for my work  as well it's not just entertainment to  me so honestly it was like a really hard  big pill to try to swallow so let's talk  about what happened  so for the first like week or so I found  myself constantly like picking up my  phone checking for notifications like  swiping up to see what was going to be  able to give me a little bit of a  dopamine hit and when there wasn't  anything there because all those social  media apps were gone and like emails  kind of boring it's just work would be  coming my way which is never fun I felt  this like gaping hole which was really  an interesting realization for me so I  had of like boredom I guess in  desperation for something on my phone I  ended up downloading like a bunch of  crossword apps and little games to play  just something to like keep my mind and  hands and stuff busy but just weird I  ended up looking at a lot of photos like  scrolling through photos making dinner  reservations or like looking at dinner  reservations for date nights I just like  don't have time for I don't know a  babysitter for what else was I doing  oh I was like going on like shopping  stores like online shopping stores and  like Amazon Bank Lee didn't buy too much  I was worried about that but yeah it is  amazing what a habit it is for us to  constantly holding and looking at our  phone and this little experiment here  really proved that to me so after seven  days or so I found the urge to like pick  up my phone and and do something random  definitely had decreased I still  occasionally would like text somebody  totally randomly but it was definitely  not as strong a drive as what I was  experiencing in those first few days  that was surprising to me but was more  surprising and beneficial was some of  the outcomes I experienced as a result  of going through this experiment so  first of all I think I became a much  better mom now without the distraction  of like my notifications going off or  even just like the drive to pick up my  phone and look at it all the time I felt  like I was able to bond so much more  with my little 2